---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Contact | 1rmaxcalculator.com';
const description = 'Get in touch with the team at 1rmaxcalculator.com for questions, corrections, or feedback on calculators and guides.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Contact</h1>
  <p>Questions, corrections, or suggestions about a calculator or guide? We'd like to hear from you. Email <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a> and we'll respond within a few business days.</p>

  <h2>What we respond to</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Formula corrections.</strong> If you believe one of our 1RM formulas is documented incorrectly, our Wilks coefficient constants are wrong, or a calculation produces a result that doesn't match the published research, let us know. Include the specific page, the input values, and the expected output and we'll investigate promptly.</li>
    <li><strong>Errors or unclear language in guides.</strong> Strength training advice is not one-size-fits-all, and we try to acknowledge nuance and limitations in our content. If a guide contains an inaccuracy, omits an important caveat, or is unclear in a way that could lead to poor training decisions, we want to know.</li>
    <li><strong>Suggestions for new calculators or guide topics.</strong> If there's a strength training calculation we don't cover — a specific program's percentage tables, a recovery metric, a competition-scoring system — we welcome suggestions and track them for future content.</li>
    <li><strong>Technical issues with the calculator.</strong> If the 1RM calculator, Wilks calculator, or any other interactive tool fails to load or produces unexpected results, please include your browser, device, and the input values you were using when you report the issue.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we don't respond to</h2>
  <p>We can't provide individualized training coaching, program design, or medical advice. Our calculators and guides are general educational tools — they describe how formulas work and how evidence-based training principles apply in typical cases. For your specific training situation, work with a qualified strength and conditioning coach (NSCA CSCS, USAW, or similar credential). For health or injury questions, consult a licensed sports medicine physician or physical therapist. We also don't respond to partnership solicitations or paid content placement requests.</p>
</BaseLayout>
